# Fried Rice

Based on [http://www.geniuskitchen.com/recipe/…](http://www.geniuskitchen.com/recipe/chinese-fried-rice-38748) ([wayback](http://web.archive.org/web/20190403104605/https://www.geniuskitchen.com/recipe/chinese-fried-rice-38748)), [https://www.loveonaplate.net/hoisin-fried-rice/…](https://www.loveonaplate.net/hoisin-fried-rice/#wprm-recipe-container-5520) ([wayback](http://web.archive.org/web/20250818162132/https://www.loveonaplate.net/hoisin-fried-rice/)), and [https://www.cookwell.com/recipe/…](https://www.cookwell.com/recipe/restaurant-style-chicken-fried-rice) ([wayback](http://web.archive.org/web/20250916134137/https://www.cookwell.com/recipe/restaurant-style-chicken-fried-rice))

{ rating="4" image="fried_rice_hoisin.jpeg" }
:::
:::

## Ingredients

Combine:

- [ ] 2 cups [uncooked rice]{ ing="uncooked-rice" } (white, brown, or day-old leftover rice)
- [ ] (Optional) [protein]{ ing="protein" } (chicken, shrimp, tofu, etc.), see notes below
- [ ] [finely chopped onion]{ ing="finely-chopped-onion" }, carrots, broccoli
- [ ] 1-2 bag(s) [frozen oriental vegetables]{ ing="frozen-oriental-vegetables" }

      - or fresh Onion, carrots, mushrooms, etc. cut in small diced
- [ ] 1/2 bag [frozen peas]{ ing="frozen-peas" }
- [ ] 3 [eggs]{ ing="egg" }
- [ ] 1 Tbsp [soy sauce]{ ing="soy-sauce" }
- [ ] 1 Tbsp [Sriracha]{ ing="sriracha" }
- [ ] 1 tsp [sesame oil]{ ing="sesame-oil" }
- [ ] 1/2 tsp [sugar]{ ing="sugar" }
- [ ] (Optional) 3g MSG or [chicken bouillon powder]{ ing="chicken-bouillon-powder" }
- [ ] (Optional) [Hoisin Sauce]{ ing="hoisin-sauce" } (if using, replaces everything except soy sauce)
- [ ] 2 [garlic cloves]{ ing="garlic-cloves" }, minced
- [ ] (Optional) [ginger]{ ing="ginger" } (~1 tsp ground)
- [ ] (Optional) [toasted sesame seeds]{ ing="toasted-sesame-seeds" }
- [ ] (Optional) 4 [green onions]{ ing="green-onions" }, chopped
- [ ] (Optional) [bean sprouts]{ ing="bean-sprouts" }

## Recipe

1. Prep 2 cups of [rice]{ ing="uncooked-rice" }. Set aside
1. On medium-high heat with a small amount of [sesame oil]{ ing="sesame-oil" }, cook the [protein]{ ing="protein" }(s). Then set aside in a bowl
1. Microwave the frozen [vegetables]{ ing="frozen-oriental-vegetables" } for less than the recommended time by at least 1 minute to avoid overcooking. Cut off a small corner of the bag and drain any liquid. Set aside in the bowl

   1. If the [vegetables]{ ing="frozen-oriental-vegetables" } can’t be microwaved, saute in a small amount of [oil]{ ing="sesame-oil" } and drain before adding other ingredients
   1. _(If using fresh [vegetables]{ ing="frozen-oriental-vegetables" }, saute them with the garlic)_
1. In parallel, in a small pan, scramble the [eggs]{ ing="egg" }
1. Heat the garlic and [ginger]{ ing="ginger" } until aromatic (~30s), then add the [proteins]{ ing="protein" } and [vegetables]{ ing="frozen-oriental-vegetables" }. Cook until warmed

   1. Add the [rice]{ ing="uncooked-rice" }, [soy sauce]{ ing="soy-sauce" }, [Sriracha]{ ing="sriracha" }, [sugar]{ ing="sugar" }, and other seasonings and mix well
   1. Mix in the [eggs]{ ing="egg" } and optional toppings ([green onion]{ ing="green-onions" }, beans [sprouts]{ ing="bean-sprouts" }, etc)

## Notes

- **Rice options:** Day-old refrigerated rice works best for fried rice (firmer texture, less sticky). Brown rice can be used for higher fiber and lower glycemic impact. Fresh rice tends to clump and get mushy
- **Flavor/texture tradeoff with brown rice:** Brown rice is chewier and less sticky than white rice, which actually works well for fried rice. The nuttier flavor complements the sesame oil and soy sauce. Cook time is the same once the rice is already cooked
- **Lower carb option:** Use a 50/50 blend of cooked rice and riced cauliflower. Add the cauliflower rice after the regular rice to avoid overcooking it (1-2 minutes is enough)
- **Best practice for glycemic control:** Use day-old rice that has been refrigerated. The cooling process creates resistant starch, which has a lower glycemic impact. Reheat gently before using
- **Protein flexibility:** This recipe works with any protein. Keep a variety of cooked proteins on hand for quick variations throughout the week

### Protein Preparation Notes

1. For chicken, chop into small cubes and place into a bowl. Mix together salt, sugar, soy sauce, and oil and marinate for 15 minutes.

   1. Boneless chicken thighs (~1/3 lb)
   1. Marinade: 2g salt, 4g sugar, 10g soy sauce, 5g oil
